  measurementId: '<MEASUREMENT_ID>',
  debug: true
});
```

2.  **Use the Firebase Console**: Next, navigate to the Firebase console and select the project you're interested in. Click on the "Realtime Database" or "Firestore" tab, depending on which database you're using. Then, click on the "Debug" tab. This will show you a list of all the reads, writes, and deletes made to your database.

3.  **Use the Firebase SDK**: You can also use the Firebase SDK to instrument your code and track reads. For example, in JavaScript, you can use the `firebase.database().goOffline()` and `firebase.database().goOnline()` methods to track when your app goes online and offline. This can help you identify which parts of your app are causing the high read counts.

    ```javascript
firebase.database().goOffline();
// Your app code here
firebase.database().goOnline();
```

4.  **Use the Firebase Performance Monitoring**: Firebase Performance Monitoring allows you to track the performance of your app, including the number of reads made to your database. You can use this data to identify which parts of your app are causing high read counts.

5.  **Use the Firebase DebugView**: The Firebase DebugView is a tool that allows you to inspect the data being sent to and received from your Firebase Realtime Database or Cloud Firestore. This can help you identify which parts of your app are causing high read counts.

6.  **Use the Firebase SDK to track reads**: You can use the Firebase SDK to track the number of reads made to your database. For example, in JavaScript, you can use the `firebase.database().ref().on('value')` method to track when a value is read from your database.

    ```javascript
var ref = firebase.database().ref('path/to/data');
ref.on('value', function(snapshot) {
  console.log('Read count:', snapshot.numChildren());
});
```

By following these steps, you should be able to instrument your Firebase calls and identify the source of high read counts in your app.
